  "notes_context": "These notes were distilled from earlier work in this repository that passed verification. Follow them unless the code or CHANGELOG.md shows they no longer hold.\n- [at-0001] Round money only with round_money: Always round monetary values with ledgerly.money.round_money (half-up to the cent); never use Python's round() or a bare Decimal.quantize(), which use banker's rounding and produce wrong invoice totals.\n  (learned from task E01 on ledgerly 1.0.0; relied on 0x, 0 passed, 0 failed)\n- [at-0002] Normalize unit prices with to_money and ZERO: In ledgerly/invoice.py, wrap raw line unit prices with ledgerly.money.to_money and seed sums with ledgerly.money.ZERO, as line_total and invoice_total already do.\n  (learned from task E01 on ledgerly 1.0.0; relied on 0x, 0 passed, 0 failed)\n- [at-0003] Raise BillingError with stable codes: Reject invalid input by raising `BillingError(code, detail)` from `ledgerly.errors` with the established codes: `invalid_state` for wrong invoice status, `invalid_quantity` for bad quantities, `negative_amount` for negative money.\n  (learned from task E02 on ledgerly 1.0.0; relied on 0x, 0 passed, 0 failed)\n- [at-0004] Exclude bool from integer checks: When validating integer fields, reject `bool` explicitly (`isinstance(x, bool) or not isinstance(x, int)`) because Python treats `True`/`False` as ints.\n  (learned from task E02 on ledgerly 1.0.0; relied on 0x, 0 passed, 0 failed)",
